  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Elevated tide cycles persist due to Post- \nTropical Cyclone Josephine well off the coast, resulting in \nwidespread minor coastal flooding continuing in Ocean County, NJ\nand Sussex County, DE.\n\nAs Post-Tropical Cyclone Josephine lingers well off the coast \nelevated tide cycles are persisting some areas. \n\nIn Ocean County, New Jersey and Sussex County, Delaware, water \nlevels remain elevated and minor tidal flooding is continuing, \nparticularly in the back bays. In fact a few locations in the \nback bays came very close to or even touched moderate levels of \ncoastal flooding yesterday with the high tide. It's going to \ntake some time for water levels to diminish in these areas so \nwe've continued the Coastal Flood Advisory for these counties \nthrough this evening. Elsewhere along the Atlantic coast and \nDelaware Bay any additional coastal flooding looks to be spotty \nat most. \n\nAt this time we are not forecasting any coastal flooding for \nthe Maryland eastern shore. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Frost and freeze impacts possible tonight and \nSaturday night where the growing season continues. \n\nAs an upper-level trough pivots across the Northeast today, a  \nstrong mid-level ridge in the Plains to Midwest shifts eastward \nsome into the weekend. This will support strong surface high \npressure shifting eastward into the Northeast and Mid-Atlantic \nthrough the weekend.\n\nA chilly and dry air mass will accompany this strong surface \nhigh, and as the high itself settles more overhead, the winds \nwill decouple. This will set the stage for efficient radiational\ncooling both tonight and Saturday night. The forecast lows are \nin the 30s for much of the area (away from the coasts and \noutside of the urban centers) with a frost and freeze potential.\nThere is some potential for winds to stay a bit elevated tonight\nso opted to maintain the existing Freeze Watch for locations NW\nof the I-95 corridor where the growing season remains active. \nFrost Advisories may be needed outside of these areas for \ntonight, especially if the radiational cooling ends up being \nstronger. Another round of near freezing lows is possible \nSaturday night when additional headlines may be necessary.",
